            <ArticleTitle>Azithromycin Pharmacodynamics against 'Persistent' Haemophilus influenza in Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">AAC.01995-17</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1128/AAC.01995-17</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The pharmacodynamic profile of azithromycin against persistent strains of non-typeable H. influenzae (NTHi) from COPD patients was characterized. Azithromycin displayed differential concentration-dependent activity (R2 &gt;0.98): pharmacodynamic response was attenuated comparing the 'first' vs. 'last' strain of NTHi which persisted in the airway of the same patient for 819 days (EC50 increased &gt;50 times: 0.0821 mg/L vs. 4.23). In the Hollow Fiber Infection Model NTHi viability was maintained throughout simulated Zithromax Z-pak regimens over 10 days.</AbstractText>
